1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,450
Elias's newspaper was dated
tomorrow.

2
00:00:02,873 --> 00:00:03,663
He lived alone.

3
00:00:03,773 --> 00:00:04,883
He had no subscription.

4
00:00:05,325 --> 00:00:07,635
A misprint — until page nine
reported

5
00:00:07,635 --> 00:00:08,515
his death.

6
00:00:08,834 --> 00:00:09,704
Apartment fire.

7
00:00:09,704 --> 00:00:11,864
Friday. Three a.m.

8
00:00:11,864 --> 00:00:12,514
His name.

9
00:00:12,784 --> 00:00:14,534
He booked a hotel across town.

10
00:00:14,958 --> 00:00:16,628
Saturday's paper was at the
front

11
00:00:16,628 --> 00:00:18,943
desk. The fire happened anyway.

12
00:00:19,173 --> 00:00:19,883
Same building.

13
00:00:20,033 --> 00:00:20,823
Same hour.

14
00:00:20,873 --> 00:00:21,977
Different man.

15
00:00:21,987 --> 00:00:23,697
The dead man was his neighbour,

16
00:00:23,697 --> 00:00:25,277
who'd knocked to warn him.

17
00:00:25,508 --> 00:00:27,618
Elias spread every paper he'd
saved

18
00:00:27,618 --> 00:00:28,778
across the floor.

19
00:00:29,133 --> 00:00:30,163
All dated tomorrow.

20
00:00:30,393 --> 00:00:31,923
Each mentioned him less than the

21
00:00:31,923 --> 00:00:33,950
last. First his job.

22
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:34,770
Then his friends.

23
00:00:34,770 --> 00:00:36,230
Then the crossword.

24
00:00:36,510 --> 00:00:38,660
By Friday, his photograph was a

25
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:39,540
grey rectangle.

26
00:00:39,961 --> 00:00:41,971
Saturday's paper arrived blank.

27
00:00:42,181 --> 00:00:43,711
Only the masthead remained.

28
00:00:44,213 --> 00:00:46,403
The Midnight Archive: all the
news

29
00:00:46,403 --> 00:00:47,503
that never was.

30
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:49,710
Elias finally understood.

31
00:00:49,710 --> 00:00:51,970
It wasn't predicting his future.

32
00:00:52,310 --> 00:00:53,780
It was deciding what to keep.
